That’s interesting. Let me continue to see what happens after that.

Seventh term:

\( a_7 = \) (sum of digits of \( a_6 \)) × 13

But \( a_6 \) is 130 again, so same digits as \( a_3 \).

Sum of digits of 130 again is \( 1 + 3 + 0 = 4 \), so:

\( a_7 = 4 \times 13 = 52 \)

Ah, so \( a_7 = 52 \), which is the same as \( a_4 \).

So, looking back:

\( a_1 = 934 \)

\( a_2 = 208 \)

\( a_3 = 130 \)

\( a_4 = 52 \)

\( a_5 = 91 \)

\( a_6 = 130 \) → same as \( a_3 \)

\( a_7 = 52 \) → same as \( a_4 \)

\( a_8 \) would be (sum of digits of \( a_7 = 52 \)) ×13, so same as \( a_5 \):

Sum of digits of 52: 5+2=7, so 7×13=91, so \( a_8 = 91 \), same as \( a_5 \)

\( a_9 \): sum of digits of 91 = 9+1=10, so 10×13=130, so \( a_9 = 130 \), same as \( a_3 \) and \( a_6 \)

Therefore, starting from \( a_3 \), we can see that the sequence enters a cycle of length 3: 130, 52, 91, 130, 52, 91, and so on.

